  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe upper level low that has been hanging around all weekend\nwill exit the area today with weak high pressure at the surface\nand aloft over the area today.\n\nCurrently, scattered light showers were situated over the far\nsouth central portion of the state. Although the showers were\npropagating westward around the mid-upper level circulation, the\nentire system continues to track off to the east. In the north,\na shortwave impulse was producing shower activity along the\nHighway 2 corridor in northwest/north central ND. There have\nbeen a few areas of higher reflectivity (showers) and Minot has\nseen some measurable precipitation so will mention some isolated\nto scattered showers prior to 12Z, with isolated sprinkles this\nmorning.\n\nSkies were mostly cloudy and so far there has been little if \nany development of fog. Still have a mention of some patchy fog \nin the far north and far south through mid morning and hoping\nthis will hold. There does look to be some fog over southern \nCanada and if skies clear in the far north, could still see some\npatchy dense fog develop. Will monitor.\n\nFor today. Look for mostly cloudy to cloudy skies this morning\nwith clouds decreasing during the day. It will be cool again,\nwith highs mainly in the 60s. Clear to partly cloudy tonight\nwith lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nDry on Tuesday with temperatures once again mainly in the 60s.\nWe will see increasing southerly surface winds Tuesday and\nWednesday, strongest in the west Tuesday, then central ND\nWednesday. This is due to increasing gradient winds between\nblocking high pressure to our east and developing low pressure\nin the lee of the Rockies. \n\nTuesday night through Wednesday night a shortwave trough tracks\nsouthwest to northeast across the forecast area bringing medium\nto high chances for showers to most of western and central ND.\nCurrently the NBM probability of at least a quarter inch of rain\nfrom 12Z Wednesday to 12Z Thursday is highest (60 to 70%) over \nsouth central ND, and tapers to 30-40% over southwest ND. The\nlowest probabilities (less than 20%) are currently over\nnorthwest ND, an then increase to 40 to 60% in north central ND. \nWe will see if this placement changes as we get closer to \nWednesday, but at this time it looks like a pretty broad area of\nshowers and possibly a few thunderstorms to track across the \nforecast area.\n\nUpper level ridging then builds over the forecast area late in\nthe work week and this is when we expect to see a noticeable\nwarm-up as temperatures climb into the lower 80s over portions \nof the west, with 70s over central ND. Dry conditions are in \nstore for Thursday and at least the first half of Friday. An \napproaching upper level northern stream trough and southern \nstream shortwave trough could bring low to medium precipitation \nchances later Friday and into the first half of the weekend. It \ndoes appear that temperatures will cool a little over the \nweekend, but will remain seasonable, in the 60s to lower 70s.",
